{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C006", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1984 1 Deutsche Mark from Germany. Bundesadler, value 1 between oak sprigs, DEUTSCHE MARK (Josef Bernhart). Struck in CuNi.", "new": "A 1984 West German 1 Deutsche Mark, struck in the heart of the Cold War when two Germanys faced each other across the Wall. The federal eagle — the Bundesadler, drawn by Josef Bernhart — had watched over this denomination since the currency reform of 1948 that created the Deutschmark and, with it, the West German economic miracle. By 1984 the DM was one of the world's hardest currencies and this copper-nickel workhorse, over 32 million struck, passed through millions of hands in Helmut Kohl's West Germany, just five years before the Wall fell and the coin's world changed forever.", "source": "Numista N#320 (Germany 1 Mark 1950-2001, KM#110, Josef Bernhart eagle design, CuNi 5.5g 23.5mm); German Federal Bank history of the Deutsche Mark 1948", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/DE.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C222", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1965 1 yen from Japan. Young tree / 日本国, 一円 // big 1, Showa regnal-year date (昭和 40 = 1965, 51 = 1976). Struck in aluminum.", "new": "A 1965 Japanese 1 yen — Showa 40 — struck at the peak of Japan's postwar economic miracle. The young tree on the face is a deliberate emblem of a country growing again: just a year after the 1964 Tokyo Olympics announced Japan's return to the world stage, and in the middle of the Ikeda government's income-doubling decade, when the economy grew at around 10 percent a year. The featherweight aluminum coin, barely a gram, was introduced in 1955 and has barely changed since; this one is among more than 1.7 billion struck in 1965 alone.", "source": "Numista N#383 (Japan 1 yen 1955-, aluminum 1g 20mm, young tree design); Bank of Japan history of postwar coinage; Showa 40 = 1965 regnal year", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/JP.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C265", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1950 5 Pfennig from Germany. Oak seedling reverse (Adolf Jäger), value between rye ears, PFENNIG obverse. Struck in brass-plated steel.", "new": "A 1950 5 pfennig from the brand-new Federal Republic of Germany — among the very first coins of the Deutschmark era. Adolf Jäger's oak seedling, a tiny tree pushing up between rye ears, was chosen as the emblem of a country starting over from rubble, and it stayed on the pfennig for the next half century. Struck in brass-plated steel in the year the Korean War boom began pouring orders into West German factories, this coin is pocket change from the first chapter of the Wirtschaftswunder; over 1.1 billion of its kind were minted in 1950.", "source": "Numista N#860 (Germany 5 Pfennig 1949-2001, KM#107, Adolf Jäger oak seedling, first DM coinage 1948-50); Deutsche Bundesbank on the 1948 currency reform", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/DE.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C035", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1971 50 Pfennig from Germany. Trümmerfrau planting oak sapling (Richard M. Werner and Gerda Werner), Bundesadler value side. Struck in CuNi 75/25.", "new": "A 1971 West German 50 pfennig honoring the Trümmerfrauen — the 'rubble women' who cleared Germany's bombed cities by hand after 1945. The design, by Richard M. Werner from a sketch modeled on his wife Gerda, shows a woman planting an oak sapling: ruin turned deliberately into renewal. It is one of the most humane designs ever put on a circulating coin, and it ran from 1949 until the euro arrived. This 1971 piece was struck as Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik was reshaping West Germany's place in a divided Europe, with over 92 million minted that year.", "source": "Numista N#847 (Germany 50 Pfennig 1949-2001, KM#109.1, Trümmerfrau by Richard M. Werner, CuNi 3.5g); German historical record of the Trümmerfrauen", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/DE.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C104", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1996 25 cents from Netherlands. The obverse shows Beatrix portrait; the reverse shows squared grid field with large 25; ct, date. Struck in nickel.", "new": "A 1996 Dutch 25 cents — a 'kwartje' — from the last years of the guilder. The squared, modernist grid with the big 25 is pure 1980s Dutch design, and the portrait is Queen Beatrix, who reigned from 1980 to her abdication in 2013. The kwartje had been the Netherlands' favorite small coin for generations, and this nickel piece, nearly 25 million struck at Utrecht, circulated for only six more years: euro cash replaced the guilder in January 2002, and the kwartje became a keepsake.", "source": "Numista N#1172 (Netherlands 25 cents 1980-2001, Beatrix, nickel 3g, Utrecht mint); De Nederlandsche Bank on guilder-to-euro changeover 2002", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/NL.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C239", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1944 1 penny from Australia. The obverse shows George VI; the reverse shows kangaroo right; AUSTRALIA, PENNY. (Perth dot), 1944, star. Struck in bronze.", "new": "A 1944 Australian penny, struck at Perth while the Pacific war raged to Australia's north. The bronze kangaroo reverse, designed by George Kruger Gray, had bounded across Australian coppers since 1938, but wartime pennies are scarce: metal was short and mintages collapsed, and this Perth issue — marked by the small dot after PENNY — saw just over 2.1 million struck, against tens of millions in peacetime years. It is small change from the year Australia, with American allies, turned the tide in New Guinea.", "source": "Numista N#8006 (Australia penny 1938-64, KM#36, kangaroo by George Kruger Gray, Perth dot mint mark, 1944 mintage 2,112,000); Perth Mint wartime history", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/AU.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C219", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2015 1 dollar from Jamaica. The reverse shows arms; crocodile crest, Taino supporters, JAMAICA, ONE DOLLAR, 2015, heptagon. Struck in nickel-plated steel.", "new": "A 2015 Jamaican one dollar, heptagonal and bright, carrying the full achievement of Jamaica's national arms. The crocodile crest on top is one of the most distinctive in world heraldry, and the two Taino supporters — an Arawak man and woman — honor the island's first people, beneath the motto 'Out of Many, One People.' Jamaica adopted the dollar in 1969, replacing the pound, and the seven-sided dollar coin has been the workhorse of the currency ever since independence-era coinage gave way to modern issues.", "source": "Numista N#22208 (Jamaica 1 dollar, heptagonal, national arms with crocodile crest and Taino supporters); Bank of Jamaica on national symbols and 1969 decimalization", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/JM.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C250", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1967 20 cents from Singapore. Value side rice stalks, large 20, CENTS, 1967, SINGAPORE, national arms and lion-fish on the obverse. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "A 1967 Singapore 20 cents — a first-year coin of the Singapore dollar itself. When Singapore separated from Malaysia in 1965, it needed its own money, and the new dollar arrived in 1967 with this cupronickel series: rice stalks for the region's agriculture, the national arms on the other face. Lee Kuan Yew's government was just beginning the transformation of a small trading port into an industrial state, and 36.5 million of these twenty-cent pieces went out into that new economy in its first year.", "source": "Numista N#4995 (Singapore 20 cents 1967-85, KM#5, first SGD coinage 1967, cupronickel); Monetary Authority of Singapore on the 1967 currency issue", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/SG.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C164", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1932 British halfpenny. The front shows King George V facing left, by Bertram Mackennal; the back shows Britannia seated with her trident. Struck in bronze.", "new": "A 1932 British halfpenny from the depths of the Great Depression. George V, engraved by Bertram MacKennal, faces left as he had since 1911, while Britannia sits with her trident on the reverse — a design lineage stretching back to Roman coins. 1932 was the year after Britain was forced off the gold standard, with unemployment above 20 percent in the industrial north; over 14 million of these bronze halfpennies were struck anyway, the small change of hard times. The halfpenny survived until decimalization killed it in 1969.", "source": "Numista N#1667 (UK halfpenny 1911-36, George V by Bertram MacKennal, seated Britannia, bronze, 1932 mintage 14,448,000); Royal Mint decimalization history", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/GB.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C246", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1971 10 sentimos from Philippines. Portrait Francisco Baltazar (Balagtas) left, SAMPUNG SENTIMOS, large 10, arms and date on the reverse. Struck in aluminum.", "new": "A 1971 Philippine 10 sentimos honoring Francisco Baltazar — Balagtas — the great Tagalog poet whose 1838 epic 'Florante at Laura' is a cornerstone of Filipino literature. The coin belongs to the English-language 'Sampung Sentimos' series of the Bangko Sentral era, struck in nickel-brass, 80 million strong. Its date carries a shadow: 1971 was the year before Ferdinand Marcos declared martial law in September 1972, and this small coin circulated through the entire fourteen years of dictatorship that followed.", "source": "Numista N#6196 (Philippines 10 sentimos 1967-74, KM#199, Francisco Baltazar portrait, nickel-brass); Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas coinage history; 1972 martial law proclamation", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/PH.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C023", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1918 20 Pfennig from Germany. Large 20 in oak wreath, date 1918 (eagle or obverse mint letter often on the other side). Struck in iron (WWI-era emergency Notgeld family).", "new": "A 1918 German 20 pfennig struck in iron — war money in the most literal sense. By 1918 copper and nickel were reserved for shell casings, so Germany's small change was struck in iron that rusts almost as you watch; the big 20 sits in an oak wreath, a design meant to project endurance in a collapsing empire. This coin was minted in the year of the Kaiser's abdication, the November Revolution, and the armistice — the last small change of imperial Germany before the mark spiraled into the Weimar hyperinflation.", "source": "Numista N#9930 family (German Empire iron 20 pfennig WWI emergency issues, 1916-18); German wartime Notgeld history; November 1918 abdication and armistice", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/DE.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C212", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1997 50 cents from Eritrea. The obverse shows State of Eritrea arms with a camel; the reverse shows greater kudu left; STATE OF ERITREA, 1997, 50, FIFTY CENTS. Struck in nickel-plated steel.", "new": "A 1997 Eritrean 50 cents — among the first coins of one of the world's youngest currencies. Eritrea won de facto independence in 1991 and confirmed it by referendum in 1993, then introduced the nakfa in 1997, named for the town of Nakfa, a symbol of the liberation struggle. The greater kudu on the reverse and the camel on the state arms are both emblems of the country's highlands and endurance. This nickel-clad steel piece is pocket change from a nation that had waited thirty years of war for its own money.", "source": "Numista N#2410 (Eritrea 50 cents 1997, KM#47, kudu reverse, nickel-clad steel); Bank of Eritrea on the 1997 nakfa introduction; 1993 independence referendum", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/ER.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C160", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1939 5 cents from Canada. The obverse shows George VI; the reverse shows beaver on rock; 5 CENTS, maple leaves, CANADA 1939 (G.E. Kruger-Gray). Struck in nickel.", "new": "A 1939 Canadian five cents with George Kruger Gray's beaver, struck in the year the world went back to war. The beaver — Canada's national animal since 1975 officially, but a fur-trade emblem for centuries — sits on its rock as it had since the design debuted in 1937. Canada declared war on Germany on September 10, 1939, a week after Britain, and this nickel five-cent piece, one of 5.6 million minted at Ottawa, is small change from the last months of peace.", "source": "Numista N#121 (Canada 5 cents 1937-42, KM#33, beaver by G.E. Kruger-Gray, nickel, 1939 mintage 5,661,123); Royal Canadian Mint; Canada's September 10, 1939 war declaration", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/CA.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C047", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2006 5 centavos from Brazil. The obverse shows Tiradentes portrait; the reverse shows Southern Cross globe; BRASIL, dove and Inconfidência triangle, 5 CENTAVOS, 2006. Struck in copper-plated steel.", "new": "A 2006 Brazilian 5 centavos carrying the face of Tiradentes — Joaquim José da Silva Xavier, the dentist-conspirator executed in 1792 for plotting independence from Portugal, now Brazil's great republican martyr with his own national holiday every April 21st. The reverse shows the Southern Cross globe from the national flag, and the dove-and-triangle emblem recalls the Inconfidência Mineira conspiracy. Struck in copper-plated steel during Lula's presidency, one of over 255 million minted in 2006, it is small change from the Plano Real era that finally tamed Brazil's inflation.", "source": "Numista N#7873 (Brazil 5 centavos 1998-, KM#648, Tiradentes portrait, copper-plated steel, 2006 mintage 255,488,000); Casa da Moeda; Plano Real 1994", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/BR.json"}]}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T05:10:00Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C012", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1962 1 franc from France. The obverse shows La Semeuse left (Oscar Roty); the reverse shows 1 FRANC; REPUBLIQUE FRANÇAISE, olive sprig, LIBERTE, EGALITE, FRATERNITE, date. Struck in nickel.", "new": "A 1962 French 1 franc with Oscar Roty's La Semeuse — the Sower — scattering seed against the wind, a design first cut in 1897 and revived for de Gaulle's nouveau franc of 1960. The sower is France's republican allegory of labor and hope, ringed here by Liberté, Égalité, Fraternité. This nickel piece was struck in Paris in the year the Algerian War ended with the Évian Accords, as the Fifth Republic turned the page on empire; over 14 million were minted.", "source": "Numista N#6 (France 1 franc 1960-2001, KM#925.1, La Semeuse by Oscar Roty 1897, nickel, 1962 mintage 14,014,136); Monnaie de Paris; Évian Accords March 1962", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "phase2-research", "run_id": "STORY-BATCH4-20261015", "inputs": [{"file": "data/detail/FR.json"}]}}
